(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609062100-ambient-head-shadowing-a-definition")
  (:adr/title "A language head that silently wins over a definition already using that name")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-06")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-544 kotoba-lang docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-pure-s-expression-core-and-cljk-surface.md"
                "adr-2608650000-kotoba-two-kinds-of-cannot-write"
                "adr-2608136000-six-questions-before-writing-a-check"))
  (:adr/body
  "## The shape

Twice in one day, in unrelated parts of kotoba, a language head took a name a
module had already defined, and won silently. Neither had a gate. Both were
found by measuring a claim someone had written, not by a check firing.

### 1. `ref` (kotoba-sema a2f86f87, closed 04e0c79c)

ADR-544's pure S-expression core spells a definition reference `(ref name)`.
`ref` is ALSO Clojure's STM constructor, which `lang/surface-status.edn`
`:no-ambient-mutation` forbids on the `:authority` shielding axis and
`lang/guest-grammar.edn` lists in `:forbidden-heads` beside `dosync`,
`locking`, `volatile!`, `var` and `binding`.

The pure-head rewrite runs BEFORE `:forbidden-heads` is consulted, so
admitting `ref` took that refusal away without saying so. Measured:
`(let [r (ref n)] n)` was ADMITTED at a2f86f87 where a2f86f87~2 refused it
`:kotoba.error/ambient-forbidden`.

Closed by narrowing the pure reading to the one shape the two readings do not
share -- STM's `(ref x)` takes an initial VALUE, ADR-544's `(ref name)` names
a DEFINITION -- so `(ref name)` is admitted only for a name the module's own
top level defines, and every other shape gets the security code back.

### 2. `mapv` (kotoba-sema, closed 3378b1d3)

`mapv` and `filterv` are surface aliases of `map`/`filter` in the frontend.
NEITHER is declared by the grammar authority: not in `lang/guest-grammar.edn`
`:sugar`, not in `:admitted-builtins`. They rewrote unconditionally, so
`lang/stdlib/core.kotoba`'s own `mapv` -- written over pair chains, with a
comment right above it explaining that the `map` head refuses a stored
callback -- was overridden by an alias demanding `vector-i64`.

Cost: 772 errors in `kotoba.lang.stdlib-core-oracle-test`, which was 772 of
the 786 red items in the whole kotoba-lang suite. All of them were ONE
refusal re-reported per assertion, because that namespace shares a single
`delay` and one broken form fails every test in it.

`lang/conformance/stdlib/manifest.edn` records measurements that test produced
on 2026-09-02, so it passed then. A regression, not a designed gap.

## What separates the two cases, and the rule that follows

`ref` is a DECLARED head whose other reading is forbidden. `mapv` is an
UNDECLARED alias. They need opposite treatments, and the language already has
both mechanisms -- it just had not applied either:

- The frontend's `reserved-function-names` refuses `(defn take ...)`,
  `(defn count ...)`, `(defn get ...)` outright. That is the right answer for
  a head the authority declares.
- An alias the authority does NOT declare has no claim on the name at all,
  and must yield to a definition the module actually makes.

`mapv` was in neither camp: not reserved, and not yielding. Measured
2026-09-06, it was the ONLY name where a built-in beat a user definition --
`keep`, `remove`, `sort`, `distinct`, `interpose`, `partition`, `juxt2` and
`frequencies` all already resolved to the module's own function, and the
identical body renamed to `mapv2` compiled and ran.

**Rule.** Before adding a head to the admitted set, ask whether any module
already defines that name, and whether the name means something else in the
language. If it is declared, reserve it so a definition is refused. If it is
not declared, it must lose to a definition. Silence is the defect in both
directions.

`grammar_authority_test/no-pure-head-collides-with-a-security-surface-unrecorded`
gates the first half for the pure heads: every head that also appears in a
security surface or in `:forbidden-heads` must carry a `:spelling-collision`
record. Adding `app` to `:forbidden-heads` turns it red. The second half --
undeclared aliases -- is gated by
`kotoba.compiler.mapv-alias-shadowing-test`, which pins both directions with
a control, because without the control both assertions pass for a build that
simply deleted the alias.

## A second finding, about measuring rather than about the language

Three suites in this family resolve evidence out of SIBLING checkouts through
env-overridable roots that default to `../<repo>`:

  kotoba-lang  q1-claims-are-auditable, q3-consumers-embed-the-canonical-grammar-without-drift
               KOTOBA_QUALIFICATION_ROOT / KOTOBA_COMPILER_QUALIFICATION_ROOT /
               KOTOBA_SEMA_QUALIFICATION_ROOT / KOTOBA_CORE_CONTRACTS_QUALIFICATION_ROOT
  kotoba       q8-report-is-machine-checkable-and-does-not-overclaim
               KOTOBA_LANG_AUTHORITY_ROOT / KOTOBA_COMPILER_EVIDENCE_ROOT / KOTOTAMA_EVIDENCE_ROOT
  kotoba       the-two-authority-cases-run-to-their-expected-values
               KOTOBA_LANG_AUTHORITY_ROOT, whose default is the CI path /tmp/kotoba-lang-dd8b

In a worktree without those siblings, each reports a repository that is merely
ABSENT as one that is WRONG. That is the inverse of the usual hazard -- it
fails closed rather than passing -- but it makes the suites unreadable outside
the west layout, and it cost two wrong readings in this session before the
pattern was recognised.

**Measure both sides of a comparison in the same layout, with the roots set,
or the number is about the layout and not about the tree.** Every commit in
this session records which roots were set.

## Status after this session

  kotoba-lang  399 tests / 3467 assertions / 0 failures / 0 errors
  kotoba       797 tests / 9906 assertions / 0 failures / 0 errors
  kotoba-sema  JVM 412 / 2138 / 0 / 0; nbb 257 / 1203 / 0 / 0
  grammar      16 tests / 496 assertions / 0 failures / 0 errors

ADR-544 step 1 is complete: all seven pure heads (lam app ref perform rel
query handle) are lowered by the frontend and admitted by the authority, with
the bytes carried to all five vendored copies and the digest advanced in three
repositories together.

Step 2 -- `.cljk` and pure `.kotoba` minting IDENTICAL Definition CIDs -- is
NOT done and is not claimed. The concrete gap is measured: `(app (lam [x] (+ x
1)) n)` and `(let [g (fn [x] (+ x 1))] (g n))` compile to the same wasm32
bytes and both answer 3, but their :hir-sha256 and :kir-sha256 differ, because
the synthetic binder is not the author's `g`. That needs alpha-normalisation.")))
